                "Taxonomy of Sovereign Debt Instruments: Sovereign debt instruments are divided into three core categories based on issuer and maturity structure: (1) Treasury Bills (T-Bills): Short-term sovereign zero-coupon money market debt issued by the Central Government to bridge temporary receipts and expenditure mismatches. T-Bills are issued with standard tenors of 91 days, 182 days, and 364 days (alongside Cash Management Bills for ultra-short intra-month liquidity mismatches). T-Bills pay zero periodic coupon interest; instead, they are issued at a discount to face value and redeemed at par upon maturity, carrying zero credit and zero sovereign default risk; (2) Central Government Dated Securities (G-Secs): Long-term sovereign bonds issued by the Federal Government with fixed or floating maturities spanning from 2 years up to 40 or 50 years. G-Secs typically pay semi-annual coupon interest. They encompass diverse structures, including plain-vanilla fixed-rate bonds, Floating Rate Bonds (FRBs pegged to 182-day T-Bill yields or reverse repo rates), Capital Indexed Bonds, and Sovereign Green Bonds (SGrBs) dedicated to financing public climate infrastructure; and (3) State Development Loans (SDLs): Dated securities issued by sub-national Provincial and State Governments. Although legally and constitutionally serviced through the Central Bank's consolidated revenue accounts, SDLs trade at an institutional spread of 25 to 50 basis points above Central Government G-Secs, reflecting state-level fiscal deficits, higher issuance volume, and lower secondary market trading liquidity.",
